{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Tiberias was a prominent Crusader lord who played a significant role during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was born around 1140 and belonged to the House of Buretel. His father was Humphrey II of Toron and his mother was Hodierna of Jerusalem a daughter of Baldwin II and a niece of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1: Early years and family background*\nRaymond inherited the Lordship of Tiberias from his mother upon her death in 1164. He also acquired the County of Tripoli through his marriage to Eschiva of Bures daughter of Hugh II of Bures. This union merged two key Crusader territories strengthening Raymond’s influence in the region. Raymond’s family ties were extensive he was a nephew of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and had close connections to other major noble houses in the Outremer. His mother Hodierna was a crucial figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem often acting as regent during her son Baldwin’s minority.\n\nRaymond became involved in the politics of the Crusader states early in his life. He participated in various diplomatic and military missions supporting his relatives and allies. His upbringing in a noble family exposed him to the complexities of governance and warfare in the Latin East.\n\n*highlighted section part 2: Political and military career*\nRaymond’s political career was marked by his service as Regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem in 1186. He assumed this role after the death of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond’s regency was brief lasting only a few months before Baldwin V succeeded to the throne. However his influence remained significant and he continued to advise the young king.\n\nMilitarily Raymond was noted for his participation in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 a catastrophic defeat for the Crusaders against the forces of Saladin. Raymond was not present at the battle as he was attending his sister’s wedding in Antioch. His absence was a point of controversy among his contemporaries who criticized his decision to leave the kingdom during a time of crisis. Following the Battle of Hattin Saladin captured Jerusalem and Raymond sought refuge in Tripoli.\n\nIn 1192 Raymond joined the Third Crusade led by Richard the Lionheart. He contributed significantly to the campaign by providing troops and supplies. His efforts helped secure the port of Acre which was a key Crusader stronghold. Raymond’s alliance with Richard was crucial in the redirection of Crusader efforts particularly after Richard’s capture by Leopold V of Austria.\n\n*highlighted section part 3: Later years and legacy*\nAfter the Third Crusade Raymond continued to be an active participant in the political and military affairs of the Outremer. He maintained close relations with King Richard and other Crusader leaders. His leadership and diplomatic skills were instrumental in managing the complex alliances and rivalries that characterized the region during this period.\n\nRaymond’s later years were marked by his efforts to secure the independence and stability of the County of Tripoli. He worked to strengthen the defenses of his territories and to foster good relations with local Christian and Muslim communities. His policies helped to maintain a degree of relative stability in an otherwise turbulent region.\n\nRaymond III died on January 12 1216 at the age of 75. His death marked the end of an era for the Crusader states. He was succeeded by his nephew Bohemond IV who continued the family’s legacy of leadership in the Outremer. Raymond’s contributions to the Crusades and his role in shaping the political landscape of the Latin East left a lasting impact on the history of the region. His story is a testament to the challenges and achievements of the Crusader nobility during the High Middle Ages.", "logprobs": [{